"Elmalılı states that the letters \"Ha-mim\" at the beginning of the surah are two separate names, and that this surah is considered separated in order to harmonize with the other \"Ha-mim\" surahs. Next, he says that the use of the present tense for the verb \"reveals\" indicates Allah's customary way of revelation (wahy) and points to the modes of revelation to be explained at the end of this surah (42:51). According to the author of al-Kashshaf, the meanings in this surah were revealed in their exact essence to you and to the prophets before you; for in them there is a great warning and favor for the servants.\n\nThe expression \"The heavens are almost breaking apart from above them\" describes the majesty of Allah. In the presence of His sublime majesty, the heavens tremble as if they might split open from above them. According to al-Zamakhshari, this is because Allah's exaltation is above the heavens. Next, it is stated that the angels seek forgiveness for those on the earth; this may be general, or, when specified for the believers, it carries the meaning of intercession (shafa'ah).\n\nIn verses 9–10, it is emphasized that the judgment of whatever you differ over belongs to Allah. The Messenger says, \"That is Allah, my Lord; upon Him I rely, and to Him I turn.\" Here, Elmalılı notes that the judgment will be delivered on the Day of Resurrection and that Allah will distinguish the rightful from the wrongful.\n\nIn verse 11, it is explained that Allah created the heavens and the earth, created for human beings spouses from among their own kind and pairs from the livestock, thereby providing reproduction. The expression \"There is nothing like unto Him\" is a metonymy (kinayah) emphasizing that Allah has no equal. Elmalılı points out that this expression carries the same meaning as saying \"There is nothing like Allah,\" yet the metonymy is far more emphatic. Explaining the concept of mumathalah (resemblance), he adds that Allah's attributes are not like those found in human beings, and that He is the Eternal, All-Encompassing Hearer and Seer.\n\nIn verse 12, it is explained that the keys (treasures) of the heavens and the earth belong to Allah, and that He expands and restricts provision for whom He wills. Elmalılı states that this circumstance exists through the wisdom of the All-Knowing Allah, and that there is good both in the expansion and in the restriction of provision. This verse also points to the relationship between legal rulings (shar'i rulings) and knowledge.\n\nIn verse 13, it is stated that Allah has ordained as a law for you the religion (tawhid and righteousness) that He enjoined upon Noah (Nuh), and similarly what He enjoined upon Abraham (Ibrahim), Moses (Musa), and Jesus ('Isa), which He revealed to Muhammad. The command \"Establish the religion and do not become divided therein\" is the common message of all the prophets. Defining religion, Elmalılı states that it is a path through which voluntary actions are tied to a consequence, grounded in tawhid (monotheism) and obedience. Quoting Muhyiddin Ibn 'Arabi, he explains that establishing the religion is possible through the community (jama'ah), whereas division grants an opportunity to the enemy.\n\nIn verse 14, it is stated that the reason those who fell into division after knowledge had come to them was \"baghy\" (insolence, rivalry, or envy). Elmalılı explains that knowledge alone is not sufficient, and that religious discipline is necessary to suppress the evil inclinations of the soul (nafs). Had it not been for a prior word from Allah postponing the punishment, judgment would have been rendered immediately. Then, it is added that those who were made to inherit the Scripture are also in disquieting doubt.\n\nIn verse 15, the Messenger is commanded to invite people to tawhid, to remain upright, and not to follow the whims of the people. Saying, \"I believe in whatever book Allah has sent down,\" the Messenger is tasked with judging with justice. Elmalılı emphasizes that the statement \"our deeds are ours and your deeds are yours\" leaves no room for dispute, and that the return of all is to Allah. In verse 16, it is explained that the arguments of those who dispute concerning Allah's religion after it has been accepted are invalid before their Lord, and upon them is wrath and severe punishment.\n\nIn verses 17–19, it is stated that the Book and the Balance were sent down with the truth, that the Hour might be near, that those who disbelieve seek to hasten it, whereas those who believe fear it and know that it is the truth. Elmalılı states that those who dispute concerning the Hour are in deep misguidance. In verse 19, it is emphasized that Allah is Most Subtle and Kind (Latif) to His servants and gives provision to whom He wills; it is expressed that this favor is a promise to those who believe.\n\nIn verses 20–23, it is explained that whoever desires the harvest of the Hereafter, his harvest will be increased, while whoever desires the harvest of the world, something of it will be given to him, but he will have no share in the Hereafter. Furthermore, it is stated that they have no partners who could legitimate what Allah has not permitted, and that for the wrongdoers there is a painful punishment. It is related that on the Day of Resurrection, while the wrongdoers tremble in fear, those who believe and do righteous deeds will be in the gardens of Paradise, and that this is the great favor. The Messenger is instructed not to ask for any reward for conveying the message other than love among kinfolk.\n\nIn verses 24–26, it is recounted that the polytheists said of the Messenger, \"He has invented a lie against Allah,\" whereas if Allah willed, He could seal his heart, and that He eliminates falsehood and establishes the truth. It is emphasized that Allah accepts repentance, pardons misdeeds, and knows what is done. It is added that the repentance of those who believe and do righteous deeds is accepted, while for the disbelievers there is a severe punishment.\n\nIn verses 27–29, it is stated that if Allah were to extend provision abundantly, His servants would commit tyranny, and for this reason He sends it down in the measure He wills. Sending down the rain after they have despaired and spreading His mercy everywhere are proofs of Allah's power. The creation of the heavens, the earth, and the living creatures is also among the proofs of His power, and He is fully capable of gathering them whenever He wills.\n\nElmalılı explains that the word \"harth\" means sowing crops, tilling, and yield, and that in this verse it is used for deeds and rewards. Whoever intends the reward of the Hereafter, Allah increases his harvest; and whoever desires only worldly gain, he is given of this world as much as is decreed, but he has no share in the Hereafter. This ruling is also confirmed by the hadith on intention: everyone receives a reward according to their intention, and the same act yields different outcomes with different intentions.\n\nIn verse 21, it is questioned whether the polytheists have partners who legitimate things that Allah has not permitted. These partners are devils or idols. Nothing can be legitimated without the permission of Allah. The word \"fasl\" (decisive decree) refers to the postponement of the judgment or punishment between the believers and the disbelievers until an appointed time. A painful punishment will surely come upon the wrongdoers.\n\nOn the Day of Resurrection, while the wrongdoers tremble in fear of the punishment for the injustices they committed, those who believe and do righteous deeds will be in the finest meadows of Paradise (rawdah). \"Rawdah\" refers to the most delightful areas of lush, well-watered gardens. There, the believers attain whatever they desire in the presence of their Lord; this great favor is manifold superior to worldly blessings.\n\nOur Prophet does not ask for a reward in return for his proclamation; he only asks for love due to kinship or love for the sake of closeness (qurbah) to Allah. According to Ibn Abbas, this means saying to the Quraysh, \"at least respect my kinship.\" Whoever acquires a good deed (hasanah), Allah grants him even more goodness; for He is Most Forgiving (Ghafur) and Most Appreciative (Shakur).\n\nThe polytheists claim that the Prophet invented a lie against Allah; yet if Allah willed, He could seal his heart as well. Allah eliminates falsehood and establishes the truth through His words. He accepts the repentance of those who repent. The living creatures dispersed throughout the heavens and the earth are among His signs (ayat). Allah is fully capable of gathering them all whenever He wills.\n\nThe misfortunes that befall a person are due to the sins their own hands have earned, yet Allah forgives much. It is impossible to frustrate Allah; besides Him, there is neither protector nor helper. The ships sailing like mountains upon the sea are also proof of His power. If He wills, He stills the wind, leaving the ships motionless; if He wills, He destroys them on account of sins. Those who dispute concerning the verses should know that they have no place of refuge.\n\nThe life of this world is a fleeting enjoyment; what is enduring are the blessings in the presence of Allah. Those who believe and put their trust in their Lord avoid major sins (kaba'ir) and indecencies (fawahish), and when they are angry, they forgive. They respond to the call of their Lord, establish prayer, conduct their affairs by mutual consultation (shura) among themselves, and spend out of the provision given to them. When subjected to oppression, they unite and defend their rights; and this is an obligation within the scope of communal duty (fard al-kifayah).\n\nThe recompense for an evil deed is an evil deed like it; yet whoever pardons, their reward rests with Allah. There is no blame on someone who is wronged and defends their right; the real blame is directed at those who oppress people and commit insolence on the earth without right. Patience and forgiveness are among the matters requiring great resolve.\n\nAllah speaks to a human being only in three ways: through revelation (inspiration to the heart), from behind a veil (by causing them to hear, as with Moses [Musa]), or by sending an angelic messenger. The revelation that comes through Gabriel is of the highest degree. The prophets convey the revelation they receive through these means to humanity.\n\nIn this section, the ways in which Allah sends revelation to His prophets are addressed. As stated in verse 42:51, Allah speaks to a human being in only three ways: either through direct revelation, from behind a veil, or by sending an angelic messenger. These three forms summarize all possible types of revelation. In his tafsir of this verse, Elmalılı also touches upon the lexical meaning of the concept of revelation (wahy), citing from al-Alusi that Imam Abu Abd Allah Taymi al-Isbahani stated that 'the essence of wahy is conveying, and inspiration, gesturing, and writing are also considered forms of revelation.' According to Raghib's Mufradat, however, the root essence of wahy is a swift sign, and it is used because it encompasses swiftness.\n\nAlthough revelation (wahy) lexically derives from a triliteral verb, in the Quran it generally appears in the quadriliteral 'if'al' form. The word 'iha' means both to reveal and to cause to reveal. Here, direct revelation is called 'wahy', while that which occurs through a messenger is termed 'iha'. Allah has not spoken to any prophet in any way other than these three forms and does not speak to humans face to face; for He is exalted above all. Rather, by His wisdom, He speaks through wahy or iha. Shaykh Abd al-Wahhab al-Sha'rani's statements regarding this verse are also noteworthy: what prevents one from hearing Allah's speech is the level of human nature; when the servant rises above this, Allah speaks to him as He speaks to spirits. Human nature means being entangled in affairs that hinder one from reaching the station of the spirit. To those at the level of human nature, Allah speaks through worldly things and manifests Himself in them.\n\nAmong the forms of revelation, the strongest is when the angel appears to the prophet and conveys the message. Because this serves as a witnessing between both the sender and the recipient, it is more corroborative in imparting knowledge. Revelation came to the prophets mostly in this manner. Another form is speaking from behind a veil, which is like Moses hearing the speech of Allah. The third form occurs by way of direct inspiration into the heart. The hadith of the Messenger of Allah, 'The Holy Spirit breathed into my heart,' is an example of this. In addition, inspiration, subjugation (taskhir, making subservient), and dreams also fall within the scope of revelation. Indeed, Allah inspired the honeybee and revealed to the mother of Moses. The Prophet said: 'Revelation has ceased; only glad tidings (mubashshirat) remain, and that is the dream of a believer.'\n\nRevelation is not merely a subjective emotional phenomenon; on the contrary, it possesses the quality of being a certainty of knowledge ('ilm al-yaqin) and even perceptual certainty ('ayn al-yaqin) that receives tidings from the Truth and takes place beyond the self, making things known. These three faculties (eye, ear, heart) constitute the nature of revelation. This is also consistent with the verse at the beginning of the surah: 'Thus He reveals to you, and to those before you.' Elmalılı notes here that the Quran revealed to the Messenger of Allah is a spiritual life principle, akin to the principle of knowledge and will. The Prophet did not previously know what the book was; he learned the requirements of faith only through revelation. For among the conditions of faith, reason alone is not sufficient, and there are matters that must be acquired through revelation.\n\nAs for the portion of revelation that reaches the saints (awliya) other than prophets, it is inspiration (ilham). However, this inspiration consists only of subtle secrets extending from the specific spirits of the angels; it is not the angel itself. The angel does not bring down revelation to anyone other than prophets and does not command with a divine decree. For the Sharia is complete, and only glad tidings (mubashshirat, joyous inspirations) remain. These inspirations may come from Allah to the servant directly or through an intermediary. The intermediary belongs to prophethood. The saints can only see the angel outside the state of delivering revelation; they do not see the angel at the moment of revelation. For this reason, the saints cannot attain the rank of prophethood. As al-Sha'rani also emphasized, 'The inspiration of anyone other than prophets is not a source of knowledge for the general public.' This is an established principle accepted in Islamic creed ('aqa'id) and legal methodology (usul).\n\nIn the continuation of the verse, Elmalılı conveys the following meaning: 'And thus We have revealed to you a spirit from Our command.' That is, We revealed the Quran. Previously, you did not know what the Book was, nor what faith was. But We made it a light by which We guide whom We will of Our servants. And indeed, you (O Muhammad) surely guide to the straight path, that is, the path of Allah. This path is the straight and upright way that has no crookedness or deviation, coming from Allah and leading to Allah.\n\nThis verse describes the greatness of the path of Allah and the way to attain every desire. As a concluding word, it is emphasized that whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth belongs to Allah.\n\nEverything is the property of Allah; the right of disposal and judgment belong solely to Him. For this reason, everything should be asked of Him, and should be asked within the bounds of His will, His Sharia, and His law.\n\nEven if some things are temporarily under the authority of individuals or societies, in the end all affairs return and reach Allah alone. Temporary controls cease, and judgment remains with Allah.\n\nTherefore, those who follow a path other than Allah's and worship others ultimately become bewildered and wretched. Those who keep to the path of Allah, however, attain every desire, achieving forgiveness and the pleasure of Allah.\n\nSurah ash-Shura concludes here. Following it, in the same spirit, comes Surah az-Zukhruf. With the prayer of our Prophet: 'O Allah! Make us among those whom You have guided to Your straight path.'"